  <exercises xml:id="Final" label="Final">
    <title>Final</title>
    <exercise label="Final_1">
      <statement>
        <p>In classical computing, we have an <c>AND</c> gate, comprising of two inputs and an output.
          The <c>AND</c> gate outputs a 1 if and only if <em>both</em> inputs are 1.</p>
        <p>Why can this gate not be implemented in quantum computing?</p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_2">
      <statement>
        <p> Consider the following circuit, starting in the state <m>\ket{\psi}</m>: </p>
        <image source="Final_2.png" width="50%" />
        <p> Describe the sequence of gates I can apply at the end of this circuit so that the end
          result of applying these gates is the state <m>\ket{\psi}</m>. </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_3">
      <statement>
        <p> Select the correct visual representation for a 2-qubit state that has a 20% chance of
          being measured as <m>00</m>, a 40% chance of being measured as <m>11</m>, a 20% chance of
          being measured as <m>10</m>, and the remaining chance of being measured as <m>01</m>. The <m>01</m>
          term has negative phase. </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <image source="Final_3_A.png" width="10%" />
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <image source="Final_3_B.png" width="10%" />
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <image source="Final_3_C.png" width="10%" />
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <image source="Final_3_D.png" width="10%" />
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
