    <exercise label="Final_6">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>If a state is in superposition with only 2 possible outcomes, it must be a one-qubit
          state.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_7">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you have an apparatus that can create single-qubit states in unknown (but
          consistent) superposition, always with positive phase. You also have the ability to
          measure any single-qubit state. Describe a process that you would follow to write down a
          best-guess bra-ket representation of the unknown state that this apparatus creates.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_8">
      <statement>
        <p>Select all states which are equivalent to the state <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\ket{0} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\ket{1}</m>:</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <p>
            <m>- \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \ket{0} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\ket{1}</m>
          </p>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <p>
            <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ -1\end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <p>
            <m>\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <p>
            <m>\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_9">
      <statement>
        <p>Over the next few questions, we will analyze the following circuit:</p>
        <image source="Final_9_and_beyond.png" width="75%" />
        <p>First, let us express our starting qubit states, <m>a\ket{0} + b\ket{1}</m> and <m>c\ket{0} + d\ket{1}</m> as a two-qubit state
          in bra-ket notation.</p>
        <p>Note: you can copy these symbols to express kets: |0⟩</p>
        <p>Note: if you are familiar with LaTeX, you can use dollar signs ($) to write math using
          LaTeX. You can use <c>\rangle</c> for the angled bracket at the end of kets. Using LaTeX is
          absolutely not a requirement!</p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_10">
      <statement>
        <p>A qubit is a superposition of how many values?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>0</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>1</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>2</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>unknown until measurement occurs</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_11">
      <statement>
        <p>What information is held in the quantum state?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>each possible outcome after measurement</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>phase</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>the probability of measuring one outcome over another</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>all of the above</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_12">
      <statement>
        <p>Select all possible values of a qubit after measurement.</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>0</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>1</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>a superposition of <m>\ket{0}</m> or <m>\ket{1}</m></p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_13">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>Phase is only relevant in quantum states that are in superposition.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_14">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you have a qubit in the <m>\ket{1}</m> state, and you apply a <c>Z</c> gate to the state.
          What is the resulting state?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\ket{1}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>-\ket{1}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\ket{0}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Equal superposition of <m>\ket{0}</m> and <m>\ket{1}</m> with negative phase</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
      <hint>
        <p>Think about simplifying our description of the state!</p>
      </hint>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_15">
      <statement>
        <p> Simplify the following visual representation:</p>
        <p>
          <image source="Final_15.png" width="20%" />
